## Summary

I built the production-grade SwiftUI parity of `features/system/components/state-machine/StateTimeline.tsx` — the FSM transition mini-timeline — as 11 files (2,223 Swift lines) under `apps/apple/TeslaSync/feature-views/StateTimeline.*`, following the established feature-view architecture (the `FSMTimelineChart` sibling as reference).

**What it does (full web parity + the prompt's load envelope):**
- **Pure adapter** porting `windowTransitions`, the component `useMemo` tick layout (`leftPercent`, sorted, destination-state color via the shared `FSMRegistry`), and `nextWiderPreset`.
- **State holder** (`@Observable @MainActor`, P1/S8 `StateTimelineSource` seam — no HTTP in the view), P1/S10 i18n facade, P1/S11 `view.opened` telemetry (slug `"StateTimeline"`).
- **Every state renders:** content ticks rail (tap-to-select, ring on selected, macOS hover tooltip), the web empty box with the actionable "Last transition {rel}" + **Widen window** / **Jump to last** affordances, plus loading skeleton, error+retry, stale chip+auto-refresh, offline cached chip.
- **A11y:** every tick is a labeled button (label/value/hint/isSelected); Dynamic Type, Reduce-Motion respected. All 9 source i18n keys + relative-time phrases in the catalog; no English literals in code.

**Verification (all green):** swiftlint `--strict` 0 · swiftformat `--lint` 0 · placeholder gate 0 · **34 unit tests pass** under Swift 6 strict-concurrency against the real `FSMRegistry` · view layer **type-checks** against the real design tokens (iOS 18 sim, Swift 6).

**Note — gate `STATUS=BLOCKED` (environmental, not this surface):** The whole-app `xcodebuild build test` cannot go green here: iOS hits a **pre-existing Xcode 26.5 IRGen compiler crash in `AppearanceSettings.Controls.swift`** (outside my allowed files) — **proven environmental** because a baseline build with all my files removed crashes with the identical signature; macOS fails at code signing (no dev certificate). I had to prefix supporting types `FSMStateTimeline*` to avoid colliding with the existing `StateTimelineWidget` in the same module. Full evidence is in `.github/prompts/monorepo/logs/p4-apple-feature-views-0235-StateTimeline.log`. Committed as `a3f91c0ce`.



